    "updated_at": "2023-04-28T01:20:00Z",
    "body": "OK, so, I wrote some tests: https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/39516 .\r\n\r\nChromium does behave as I expected, and does replace-before-load based on the target navigable.\r\n \r\nBut both Gecko and WebKit do *not* do any replace-before-load behavior here. (Including if you replace before the form document finishes loading.) So we have the option of saying that cross-window form submission never changes from \"push\" to \"replace\". The spec text would be something like replacing\r\n\r\n> If _form document_ has not yet [completely loaded](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#completely-loaded), then set _historyHandling_ to \"[replace](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#hh-replace)\".\r\n\r\nwith\r\n\r\n> If _form document_ equals _targetNavigable_'s active document, and _form document_ has not yet [completely loaded](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#completely-loaded), then set _historyHandling_ to \"[replace](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#hh-replace)\".\r\n\r\nI tend to prefer Gecko and WebKit's behavior here, especially because of how it simplifies the cross-origin (and thus cross-process) case. @natechapin, what do you think, are you up for changing Chromium to follow Gecko and WebKit? @petervanderbeken, what do you think of this solution?\r\n\r\nEDIT: upon writing tests for the opposite behavior, it seems like I don't quite understand what Gecko and WebKit are doing. They pass neither set of tests. But regardless, the rest of this thread indicates multi-implementer interest in converging on the proposal above, of preventing replace-before-load behavior in cross-document targeted=\"\" form submissions, so we can all steer toward that simpler outcome.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
